This example demonstrates **Docker custom bridge networking** and container-to-container communication by **container name**.

### Step 1 — Create a custom bridge network

```bash
docker network create --driver bridge my_bridge
```

This creates a user-defined Docker bridge network called:my_bridge

### Step 2 — Run two containers on the same network
docker run -dit --name app1 --network my_bridge alpine sh
docker run -dit --name app2 --network my_bridge alpine sh
Now the topology is:
Docker Host
     |
  my_bridge
   /     \
app1     app2
Both containers are attached to the same custom bridge network.

### Step 3 — Test communication using the container name

Enter `app1`:
docker exec -it app1 sh
Then from inside `app1`: ping app2
Docker's built-in DNS resolves:
app2
  ↓
app2's container IP
So you don't need to know the IP address of `app2`.

### Why this is useful
Docker's embedded DNS handles the name resolution.

### Important distinction - A user-defined bridge network is better for application communication than the default `bridge` network because containers attached to the same user-defined network can discover each other by container name.

The core idea from your image is:

Create network
      ↓
Attach app1 + app2
      ↓
app1 calls "app2"
      ↓
Docker DNS resolves "app2"
      ↓
Container-to-container communication

So the interview-ready statement is:

A Docker custom bridge network allows containers on the same host to communicate with each other using 
container names through Docker's embedded DNS.
